EXAMPLES=[
 {'id':'we-2-source-28','num':'28','title':'Example 28','problem':r'If \(f(x)=x^2+2x+3\) and \(g(x)=x-4\), calculate \(\lim_{x\to2}[f(x)+g(x)]\).','given':'Two polynomials, approaching x = 2.','method':'Apply the sum rule and evaluate the polynomial limits.','steps':['Polynomials are continuous, so limₓ→2 f(x) = f(2) = 4 + 4 + 3 = 11.','Similarly, limₓ→2 g(x) = g(2) = 2 − 4 = −2.','The sum rule gives limₓ→2 [f(x) + g(x)] = 11 + (−2) = 9.','Check by adding first: f(x) + g(x) = x² + 3x − 1. Substituting x = 2 gives 4 + 6 − 1 = 9.'],'answer':'9.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':49,'pageEnd':49,'verification':'question and printed solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-2-source-29','num':'29','title':'Example 29','problem':r'If \(f(x)=x-7\) and \(g(x)=x^2+3x+2\), calculate \(\lim_{x\to1}[f(x)-g(x)]\).','given':'Two polynomials, approaching x = 1.','method':'Apply the difference rule, keeping the subtraction sign on every term.','steps':['Evaluate the first limit: f(1) = 1 − 7 = −6.','Evaluate the second limit: g(1) = 1 + 3 + 2 = 6.','The difference rule gives limₓ→1 [f(x) − g(x)] = −6 − 6 = −12.','Check by subtracting: (x − 7) − (x² + 3x + 2) = −x² − 2x − 9. At x = 1, this is −1 − 2 − 9 = −12.'],'answer':'−12.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':49,'pageEnd':50,'verification':'question and complete printed solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-2-source-30','num':'30','title':'Example 30','problem':r'If \(f(x)=x+5\) and \(g(x)=2x-4\), calculate \(\lim_{x\to3}[f(x)\cdot g(x)]\).','given':'Two linear functions, approaching x = 3.','method':'Apply the product rule and check by expansion.','steps':['The functions are polynomials. Their limits are f(3) = 3 + 5 = 8 and g(3) = 6 − 4 = 2.','The product rule gives limₓ→3 [f(x)g(x)] = 8 × 2 = 16.','Expand the product: (x + 5)(2x − 4) = 2x² + 6x − 20.','Substituting x = 3 into the expanded polynomial gives 18 + 18 − 20 = 16, agreeing with the product rule.'],'answer':'16.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':50,'pageEnd':50,'verification':'question and printed solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-2-source-31','num':'31','title':'Example 31','problem':r'If \(f(x)=x-4\) and \(g(x)=x^2+3\), calculate \(\lim_{x\to-2}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\).','given':'A quotient of polynomials, approaching x = −2.','method':'Check that the denominator limit is nonzero, then apply the quotient rule.','steps':['The numerator limit is f(−2) = −2 − 4 = −6.','The denominator limit is g(−2) = (−2)² + 3 = 7. It is nonzero, so the quotient rule applies.','Divide the limits: limₓ→−2 f(x)/g(x) = −6/7.','Since x² + 3 > 0 for every real x, this rational function is defined and continuous at −2. Direct substitution confirms −6/7.'],'answer':'−6/7.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':50,'pageEnd':50,'verification':'question and printed solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]}
]
